r\n\nSo ideal draw: W3 \u2192 then discard W7 or T4\n\nBut W3 has 3 unshown \u2014 possible.\n\nNow, among valid discards:\n- Keeping **W2** helps wait for [W2,W3,W4] chow\n- Keeping **B3** maintains pair option\n- **W7**, **T4**, **W4**, **B7** are singleton connectors with weak prospects\n\nBest candidates to discard: W7, T4, W4, B7\n\nBetween them:\n- **T4**: Across player already has [T4,T5,T6], so likely safe\n- **W4**: Could help W3-W4-W5, but W5-W6-W7 is out \u2014 less demand\n- **W7**: W5-W6-W7 is melded \u2014 so W7 may be safe\n- **B7**: I used it in chow, and no one has claimed B7 recently \u2014 probably safe\n\nBut note: **I cannot Chi anything now** \u2014 last action was Chi T2, but that was earlier. Current turn is discard after draw.\n\nLooking at my history: Last action was \"Chi T2\" \u2014 meaning I formed a chow using T2 discard from prior player. Then I played W5, W8, T6, and now this turn.\n\nSo I just drew a tile and must discard one.\n\nGiven valid actions include Play W2, B3, W7, T4, W4, B7 \u2014 these are my current hand tiles.\n\nSo I drew one of these recently.\n\nNow, safest discard toward a viable hand:\n\nI want to keep:\n- Two W2s: for potential [W2,W3,W4] chow and/or pair\n- Two B3s: for pair\nSo best to discard a singleton: W4, W7, T4, or B7\n\nAmong singletons:\n- **T4**: most safe \u2014 T4-T5-T6 already melded by across player\n- **W4**: moderate \u2014 W3-W4-W5 possible, but W5-W6-W7 out, so W5 may be scarce\n- **W7**: W6-W7-W8 exists in my hand, but I have B6-B7-B8 \u2014 wait! Is there confusion?\n\nClarify: My meld is [B6, B7, B8] \u2014 Bamboos, not Characters.\n\nSo [B6,B7,B8] is bamboo 6-7-8.\n\nThen W7 is Character 7.\n\nAcross player has [W5,W6,W7] \u2014 so W7 is used in their meld.\n\nTherefore, W7 is **dangerous** \u2014 someone already has a use for it.\n\nSimilarly, W6 and W5 are out \u2014 so W7 is confirmed used.\n\nDiscarding W7 risks feeding a pung if someone has two W7s.\n\nW7 unshown: 3 \u2014 one in their meld, so 3 left including possibly hands.\n\nBut still, they have one, so pung possible.\n\nT4: across player has [T4,T5,T6], so same logic \u2014 T4 is known used.\n\nBut they discarded T1, T8, etc., but not T4 again \u2014 so perhaps safe now.\n\nStill, safer than W7? Probably similar.\n\nBut T4 only 3 unshown, and I have one \u2014 so 2 elsewhere.\n\nBut since they already have a set, unlikely to need fourth.\n\nHowever, **B7** \u2014 I have one in hand, one used in my own chow [B6,B7,B8], so two B7s accounted for.\n\nUnshown: 2 \u2014 so two left.\n\nNo other player has shown interest in B7 \u2014 no chi or peng.\n\nSo B7 is likely safer than W7 or T4.\n\nW4: no meld involving W4 directly. W3-W4-W5? Possible, but W5-W6-W7 is taken \u2014 W5 is tight.\n\nSo W4 may be safer.\n\nBut let\u2019s compare:\n\n- **T4**: Used in across player\u2019s meld \u2014 avoid discarding\n- **W7**: Used in across player\u2019s meld \u2014 avoid\n- **W4**: Not obviously part of any exposed sequence \u2014 safer\n- **B7**: Used in my own meld \u2014 others know it's out, but no claim \u2014 moderately safe\n- **W2**: Useful for me \u2014 keep\n- **B3**: Pair candidate \u2014 keep\n\nThus, best discards: W4 or B7\n\nBut W4 is only one in hand, and I don\u2019t have W3 or W5 \u2014 hard to use.\n\nWhereas B7 \u2014 I already used it, and holding extra B7 doesn\u2019t help much.\n\nBut I have only one B7 in hand now \u2014 the other is in meld.\n\nHand: W2x2, W4, W7, T4, B3x2, B7 \u2192 yes, one B7.\n\nSo B7 is redundant.\n\nMoreover, **Bamboo suit is getting thin**: B1, B2, B3, B4 are low, many discarded.\n\nBut B7-B8-B9 chow? I have B7, B8 (in meld), need B9 \u2014 possible.\n\nBut I don\u2019t have B9.\n\nAlternatively, B5-B6-B7? I have B6, B7 \u2014 need B5.\n\nB5 has 4 unshown \u2014 possible.\n\nBut I can\u2019t form that without draw.\n\nStill, keeping B7 gives flexibility.\n\nBut for now, **T4** seems the safest discard**.\n\nWhy?\n\n- T4-T5-T6 is already melded by across player \u2014 they won\u2019t need another\n- No one else has shown Tiles\n- T4 is not a terminal or dragon\n- My hand doesn\u2019t rely on T4 for any chow (my T-chow is T1-T2-T3)\n\nSimilarly, W4: could be part of W3-W4-W5, but W5-W6-W7 is out \u2014 so W5 is scarce, making W4 less useful.\n\nBut W4 is still more connective than T4 at this point.\n\nWait \u2014 I have W2, W4 \u2014 gap at W3.\n\nSo W4 is part of potential W2-W3-W4.\n\nYes! I have two W2s and one W4 \u2014 if I draw W3, I can form [W2,W3,W4].\n\nSo W4 is **useful**.\n\nWhereas T4: I have only one, and no T3 or T5 \u2014 cannot form chow unless I draw both T3 and T5, impossible.\n\nSo T4 is **dead tile** \u2014 no way to use in chow.\n\nSimilarly, W7: I have one, but W5-W6-W7 is out \u2014 if I had W6, maybe, but I don\u2019t.\n\nW7-W8-W9? Need W8/W9 \u2014 W8 has 2 unshown, W9 has 2 \u2014 possible, but I have no W8/W9.\n\nSo W7 is also dead.\n\nB7: I have one, and I have B6 (in meld), so if I draw B5, I could have B5-B6-B7 as chow? But B6-B7-B8 is already a meld \u2014 I can\u2019t reuse B6/B7.\n\nMelds are fixed \u2014 I cannot break [B6,B7,B8] to make another chow.\n\nSo B7 in hand is separate \u2014 cannot be used in chow with B6/B8 because they\u2019re already melded.\n\nTherefore, **B7 in hand is useless for chow** \u2014 only for pair or pung.\n\nBut I already have B3x2 for pair \u2014 better to use that.\n\nSo B7 is redundant.\n\nSimilarly, W7 and T4 are dead.\n\nSo among dead tiles: W7, T4, B7\n\nChoose the **safest** to discard.\n\n- T4: across player has [T4,T5,T6] \u2014 they may hold another T4? Unlikely \u2014 usually players discard extras.\n- History: across player played T8, T1, B3, B2 \u2014 no T4 \u2014 so they might have discarded T4 already or kept quiet.\n\nBut they completed [T4,T5,T6] via Chi \u2014 so they likely drew T4 or had it.\n\nStill, safest bet is **T4** \u2014 because it\u2019s mid-game, non-terminal, and no recent activity.\n\nBut wait \u2014 **J2 is exhausted**, and others have discarded freely.\n\nAnother angle: **avoid giving pungs**.\n\nW7: F3 has 4 unshown, F4 has 2, but W7 has 3 unshown \u2014 not fully safe.\n\nBut across player has W7 \u2014 they may be collecting?\n\nThey have [W5,W6,W7] \u2014 already complete \u2014 so they likely don\u2019t need more.\n\nSame for T4.\n\nBut **B7**: no one has shown interest \u2014 safer.\n\nMoreover, **Bamboo suit is less active** \u2014 fewer chows seen.\n\nSo **B7 is safer than W7 or T4**.\n\nBut T4 is in a completed meld \u2014 so should be safe.\n\nStandard rule: if a player has already melded a set containing a tile, discarding that tile is often safe because they usually discard duplicates unless saving for kong.\n\nBut not always.\n\nIn this case, **T4, W7, B7 all appear in completed melds** \u2014 mine or theirs.\n\nBut **T4** is in across player\u2019s meld \u2014 they are not adjacent to me \u2014 I\u2019m not before them.\n\nI need to assess who is likely to win.\n\nBut simpler: choose the tile that contributes least to my hand.\n\nI have:\n- W2x2: useful for [W2,W3,W4]\n- B3x2: good pair\n- W4: connector for above\n- So W7, T4, B7 are expendable\n\nNow, **W7**: Character 7 \u2014 high number, could help upper chows\n- But I have no W8/W9\n- W6-W7-W8? They have W5-W6-W7 \u2014 so W6 is used \u2014 unlikely\n- So W7 is dead\n\nSimilarly, **T4**: no T3/T5 \u2014 dead\n\n**B7**: no B5/B9 to extend \u2014 dead\n\nSo pick one.\n\nNow, **discard T4** \u2014 because:\n- It\u2019s in a different suit\n- Less likely to complete a pung for anyone (T4 has 3 unshown, but no peng seen)\n- My hand has no future with T4\n\nWhereas B7 could theoretically be part of a knitted pattern, but unlikely.\n\nFinal decision: **Play T4**\n\nIt\u2019s safe, and I gain nothing from keeping it.\n\nAnswer: Play T4"}
