-W6-W7, not involving W4. Previous player has W1s. Next has T7-T8-T9. No one showing interest in W4.\n\nSimilarly, W2: no one has shown.\n\nBut W2 could be part of W1-W2-W3 or W2-W3-W4.\n\nBut no evidence.\n\nJ3 has been played by multiple players.\n\nBut among valid plays, least dangerous are W2, W4, B3.\n\nBut I must choose one.\n\nAlso consider: if I keep W2 pair, it helps All Simples (2 points), Concealed Hand (if all self-drawn), etc.\n\nBut I\u2019ve already claimed tiles (Chi B7, Chi T2), so not Fully Concealed.\n\nMelded Hand (6 points) possible \u2014 if all melds are formed by claiming, and I win by claiming the final tile.\n\nCurrently:\n- [B6,B7,B8]: formed by Chi (claimed B7)\n- [T1,T2,T3]: formed by Chi (claimed T2) \u2014 history shows \"Chi T2\"\nSo both melds are exposed and claimed.\n\nIf I form next melds by claiming, and win by claiming, I get Melded Hand (6 points).\n\nAlso, All Chows (2 points) if final meld is chow.\n\nAnd if I use W2 pair, no honor, so possible All Simples (2 points) if all tiles are 2-8.\n\nCurrent hand: W2, W2, W4, W6, W7, B3, B3, B7 \u2014 all simples (2-7), good.\n\nNeed to complete four chows and a pair.\n\nPossible chow paths:\n- With W2, W4, W6, W7: hard to form chow unless draw W3/W5.\n- With B3, B7: B3-B4-B5? But B4 only 1 unshown.\n- Or B7-B8-B9? B8 has 3 unshown, B9 has 3 \u2014 possible. But I\u2019d need B8/B9.\n\nBut I already used B8 in meld \u2014 so I have one, but can\u2019t reuse.\n\nWait: meld [B6,B7,B8] uses one B8. Three others exist. So possible.\n\nBut drawing B8 or B9 to form another chow?\n\nUnlikely.\n\nBetter to aim for character suit chow.\n\nHold W2, hope for W3 to form W2-W3-W4.\n\nW3 unshown: 3 available \u2014 possible.\n\nOr W6-W7-W8? W8 only 1 left \u2014 scarce.\n\nSo W2-W3-W4 is better target.\n\nTherefore, keep W2, W4, W6, W7.\n\nBut I must discard one.\n\nAmong valid: Play W2, W6, B3, W7, W4, B7.\n\nTo preserve flexibility, best to discard a tile not contributing to potential chows.\n\nB7 is singleton, not forming chow easily (B7-B8-B9 possible, but B8/B9 not in hand).\n\nSimilarly, B3-B4-B5 possible \u2014 B4 only 1 left.\n\nSo both B3 and B7 are weak.\n\nBut I have two B3s \u2014 so better to keep as potential pair.\n\nThus, discard B7.\n\nIt\u2019s also dangerous \u2014 since I just claimed it, others may suspect I\u2019m not saving it.\n\nWhereas W6 and W7 are hot \u2014 across player has W5-W6-W7 meld, so may want to add W6 or W7 for kong.\n\nDiscarding W6 or W7 risks Robbing the Kong if I\u2019m not winning.\n\nBut I won\u2019t win off W6/W7 now.\n\nSo safest discard is either W4 or B7.\n\nW4: not involved in known melds.\n\nB7: I just claimed it, but opponent discarded it safely \u2014 so likely not needed.\n\nBut B7 is in my hand as singleton \u2014 and I have no other B tiles except those in meld.\n\nSo B7 is useless unless I draw B6/B8 again \u2014 impossible.\n\nThus, B7 is dead tile.\n\nSimilarly, W4 is somewhat isolated.\n\nBut W4 could be part of W3-W4-W5 or W4-W5-W6.\n\nMore potential than B7.\n\nSo B7 is the worst tile to keep.\n\nTherefore, best move is to discard B7.\n\nAnswer: Play B7"}
